      "id": 1,
      "text": "Cleveland, Mississippi was named after President Grover Cleveland when settlers moved inland from the Mississippi River in 1869.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cleveland%2C_Mississippi",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Louisville, New Orleans & Texas Railroad ran through Cleveland, and the town still preserves a section of that historic line today.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cleveland%2C_Mississippi",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Dockery Plantation near Cleveland, home to blues pioneer Charley Patton, now operates as an open‑air museum free to explore.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18,
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.visitclevelandms.com/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Amzie Moore House, once the home of civil‑rights leader Amzie Moore, has been converted into a museum honoring his legacy.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cleveland%2C_Mississippi",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Delta State University, located in Cleveland, hosts the Grammy Museum Mississippi, the first Grammy Museum outside Los Angeles.",
